example.en:
Practice: In the doubly linked list 10 ⇄ 20 ⇄ 30, delete the middle node 20 and print the list forward and backward.

----------------------------------------
public class DoublyDelete {
    static class Node { int data; Node prev, next; Node(int d) { data = d; } }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Node a = new Node(10), b = new Node(20), c = new Node(30);
        a.next = b; b.prev = a; b.next = c; c.prev = b;
        Node del = b;                       // delete the middle node 20
        del.prev.next = del.next;           // 10.next = 30
        del.next.prev = del.prev;           // 30.prev = 10
        for (Node p = a; p != null; p = p.next) System.out.print(p.data + " ");
        System.out.println();
        for (Node p = c; p != null; p = p.prev) System.out.print(p.data + " ");
        System.out.println();
    }
}
----------------------------------------

Step 1: Let del = node 20. Its prev is 10 and its next is 30.
Step 2: del.prev.next = del.next makes 10.next point to 30.
Step 3: del.next.prev = del.prev makes 30.prev point to 10. Node 20 is now unlinked (Java's garbage collector frees it; in C call free).
Step 4: Forward from 10 prints 10 30; backward from 30 prints 30 10.
Answer: output **10 30** and **30 10**; only two pointer changes, O(1), because prev gives the previous node directly.

