Practice: A circular singly linked list 10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → (back to 10) is accessed through a pointer last that points to node 40. Insert 5 at the beginning and print the list.

----------------------------------------
public class CircularInsert {
    static class Node { int data; Node next; Node(int d) { data = d; } }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Node last = null;
        for (int x : new int[]{10, 20, 30, 40}) {     // build 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 40 -> (10)
            Node n = new Node(x);
            if (last == null) { last = n; n.next = n; }
            else { n.next = last.next; last.next = n; last = n; }
        }
        Node n = new Node(5);                         // insert 5 at the beginning
        n.next = last.next;                           // 5 -> 10
        last.next = n;                                // 40 -> 5
        Node p = last.next;
        do { System.out.print(p.data + " "); p = p.next; } while (p != last.next);
        System.out.println();
    }
}
----------------------------------------

Step 1: Create node N(5).
Step 2: N.next = last.next, so 5 points to 10, the current first node.
Step 3: last.next = N, so 40 now points to 5; 5 has become the first node and last is unchanged.
Step 4: Print from last.next until the walk returns to it: 5, 10, 20, 30, 40.
Answer: output **5 10 20 30 40**; the insertion is **O(1)** because the last pointer gives both ends of the circle.

