answer.en:
## Problem

Given an unsorted array of n elements and k with 1 ≤ k ≤ n, find the **k-th smallest** element, the value that would sit at index k − 1 after sorting.

## Approaches

Method | Idea | Time
Sort, then pick a[k − 1] | Any O(n log n) sort | O(n log n)
Max-heap of size k | Keep the k smallest seen so far; the root is the answer | O(n log k)
Quickselect | Partition as in quicksort, recurse into one side only | O(n) average, O(n^2) worst
Median of medians | Quickselect with a guaranteed good pivot | O(n) worst case

**Quickselect** is the usual exam answer: after one partition the pivot is at its final sorted position p. If p = k − 1 it is the answer; otherwise only the side containing k − 1 is searched.

## Program (Java, quickselect with Lomuto partition)

----------------------------------------
public class KthSmallest {
    static int partition(int[] a, int lo, int hi) {   // Lomuto: pivot = a[hi]
        int pivot = a[hi], i = lo;
        for (int j = lo; j < hi; j++)
            if (a[j] < pivot) { int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t; i++; }
        int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[hi]; a[hi] = t;
        return i;                                     // pivot's final position
    }
    static int kthSmallest(int[] a, int k) {          // 1 <= k <= n
        int lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
        while (lo <= hi) {
            int p = partition(a, lo, hi);
            if (p == k - 1) return a[p];
            if (p < k - 1) lo = p + 1; else hi = p - 1;
        }
        return -1;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] a = {7, 10, 4, 3, 20, 15};
        int k = 3;
        System.out.println(k + "rd smallest = " + kthSmallest(a, k));
    }
}
----------------------------------------

Output: 3rd smallest = 7

## Trace for a = {7, 10, 4, 3, 20, 15}, k = 3 (target index 2)

Step 1: Partition all 6 elements with pivot 15 → {7, 10, 4, 3, 15, 20}; pivot index p = 4 > 2, so hi = 3.
Step 2: Partition indices 0–3 with pivot 3 → {3, 10, 4, 7, …}; p = 0 < 2, so lo = 1.
Step 3: Partition indices 1–3 with pivot 7 → {3, 4, 7, 10, …}; p = 2 = k − 1, so stop.
Answer: the 3rd smallest element is **7** (sorted order 3, 4, **7**, 10, 15, 20).

## Notes

• The function changes the array order; pass a copy if the original order matters.
• The worst case O(n^2) happens when the pivot is always the smallest or largest; a random pivot makes it unlikely.

Real-world example: a procurement officer comparing tender bids can use the same method to find the third-lowest quoted price without sorting every bid.

Exam tip: state the time of each method, write quickselect (or at least the sort-based version) and **trace one example**; mention that k is 1-based, so the answer is at index k − 1.

