## Floyd's cycle-detection algorithm (tortoise and hare)

Use two pointers that move at **different speeds**. If the list has a loop, the fast pointer eventually **meets** the slow one inside it; if there is no loop, the fast pointer reaches NULL.

Step 1: Set slow = head and fast = head.
Step 2: While fast != NULL and fast.next != NULL, move slow one node and fast two nodes.
Step 3: If slow == fast at any point, a **loop exists**.
Step 4: If the while loop ends because fast reached NULL, there is **no loop**.
Step 5 (finding the loop's first node): set slow = head and keep fast at the meeting node; move both one node at a time. The node where they meet is the **start of the loop**.

## Java implementation

------------------------------------------
public class LoopCheck {
    static class Node { int data; Node next; Node(int d) { data = d; } }
    static Node findLoopStart(Node head) {
        Node slow = head, fast = head;
        while (fast != null && fast.next != null) {
            slow = slow.next;            // 1 step
            fast = fast.next.next;       // 2 steps
            if (slow == fast) {          // they meet inside the loop
                slow = head;
                while (slow != fast) { slow = slow.next; fast = fast.next; }
                return slow;             // first node of the loop
            }
        }
        return null;                     // fast reached NULL: no loop
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Node[] n = new Node[7];
        for (int i = 1; i <= 6; i++) n[i] = new Node(i * 10);
        for (int i = 1; i < 6; i++) n[i].next = n[i + 1];
        System.out.println(findLoopStart(n[1]) == null ? "No loop" : "Loop");
        n[6].next = n[3];                // 60 -> 30 creates a loop
        Node s = findLoopStart(n[1]);
        System.out.println(s == null ? "No loop" : "Loop starts at " + s.data);
        int len = 1;
        for (Node p = s.next; p != s; p = p.next) len++;
        System.out.println("Loop length = " + len);
    }
}
------------------------------------------

Output when run:

------------------------------------------
No loop
Loop starts at 30
Loop length = 4
------------------------------------------

## Trace for 10 -> 20 -> 30 -> 40 -> 50 -> 60 -> back to 30

No. | slow | fast | Note
0 | 10 | 10 | start
1 | 20 | 30 | slow 1 step, fast 2 steps
2 | 30 | 50 | no meeting yet
3 | 40 | 30 | fast went 50, 60, 30
4 | 50 | 50 | meet, loop found
5 | 20 | 60 | phase 2, slow restarted at head
6 | 30 | 30 | meet again, loop starts at 30

## Complexity and alternatives

• **Time** O(n), **space** O(1): only two pointers.
• **Hashing**: store each visited node in a HashSet and report a loop on the first repeat; O(n) time but **O(n) space**.
• **Marking visited nodes** with a flag also works, but it changes the node structure.

Exam tip: write the five steps, state **O(n) time and O(1) space**, and add the phase-2 step for finding where the loop starts.

